From 528e48540fc62aa0a74faf29d20a410b0fd23f06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:53:19 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 34/54] mdmon: let the metadata keep a member failure to its
 subarray

When md fails a device in one member array, mdmon fails it in every
other member array of the container (reconcile_failed() in
wait_and_act()).  That is the rule of IMSM and DDF, where a disk is
either in service or failed as a whole.

AMD RAIDXpert2 metadata (later in this series) records a failure per
volume: its driver marks failed only the element of the volume whose
I/O failed, the disk stays online and its elements in the other volumes
stay in service (seen with the AMD driver for Linux, 9.3.3: a write
error in a RAID5 failed that element alone, a RAID1 on the same disk
stayed normal).  Failing the disk everywhere degrades volumes that had
no error: a RAID1 sharing the disk with a RAID5 that took one loses it
and has to be rebuilt.

Add superswitch.fail_subarray_only: a format that sets it keeps the
failure to the member array md failed the device in.  A device that is
gone is still failed in all of them (Incremental_remove_external()),
as is one the user fails in each.  IMSM and DDF do not set it and
behave as before.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
---
 mdadm.h   | 7 +++++++
 monitor.c | 6 ++++--
 2 files changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/mdadm.h b/mdadm.h
index 6bc143c3..feae3fc7 100644
--- a/mdadm.h
+++ b/mdadm.h
@@ -1275,6 +1275,13 @@ extern struct superswitch {
 	 * grows into a RAID1), unlike IMSM, where it is the container's.
 	 */
 	int subarray_raid_disks;
+	/*
+	 * A device md fails in one subarray stays in the others: the metadata
+	 * records a failure per subarray (AMD: the element of that volume
+	 * alone), so mdmon does not fail the device in every subarray of the
+	 * container, as it does for IMSM and DDF.
+	 */
+	int fail_subarray_only;
 	const char *name; /* canonical metadata name */
 } *superlist[];
 
diff --git a/monitor.c b/monitor.c
index 7c4e4916..3a8dea70 100644
--- a/monitor.c
+++ b/monitor.c
@@ -376,7 +376,9 @@ static void signal_manager(void)
  *    device by writing '-blocked' to rd/state, and finally write 'remove' to
  *    rd/state.  Before a disk can be replaced it must be failed and removed
  *    from all container members, this will be preemptive for the other
- *    arrays... safe?
+ *    arrays... safe?  Not if the metadata records failures per member
+ *    array (fail_subarray_only): the others keep the device then.  A device
+ *    that is gone fails in all of them anyway (Incremental --fail).
  *
  *  sync completes
  *    sync_action was 'resync' and becomes 'idle' and resync_start becomes
@@ -899,7 +901,7 @@ static int wait_and_act(struct supertype *container, int nowait)
 	}
 
 	/* propagate failures across container members */
-	for (a = *aap; a ; a = a->next) {
+	for (a = *aap; a && !container->ss->fail_subarray_only; a = a->next) {
 		if (!a->container || a->to_remove)
 			continue;
 		for (mdi = a->info.devs ; mdi ; mdi = mdi->next)
